Each formula unit of lead bromide gives one lead ion and two bromide ions:


PbBr₂→ Pb^{2++2Br⁻}


Lead ions:


N(Pb²⁺)=0.15×6.022×10^{23}
=9.033×10^{22}


Bromide ions:


N(Br⁻)=0.30×6.022×10^{23}
=1.8066×10^{23}


Option A reverses the counts and ignores the 1:2 ion ratio.

---

At STP,


n(H₂)=(22.4 / 22.4)=1 mol


The molar mass of hydrogen gas is


M(H₂)=2 g,mol^{-1}


Therefore, its mass is 2 g.

Each hydrogen molecule contains two hydrogen atoms. Thus,


N(H atoms)=2×6.022×10^{23}



1.2044×10^{24 atoms}


Option C counts hydrogen molecules rather than the individual hydrogen atoms.

